    <!-- Section 4: Underwriting & DSCR -->
    <section class="content-section" id="underwriting">
      <h2>Commercial Loan Underwriting &amp; DSCR Coverage</h2>
      <p>
        Commercial banks evaluate loan applications using the <strong>5 C's of Credit</strong> (Character, Capacity, Capital, Collateral, and Conditions). The most critical financial formulas are:
      </p>

      <h3>1. Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR &ge; 1.20x &ndash; 1.35x)</h3>
      <p>
        Lenders calculate whether your business generates sufficient cash flow to cushion unexpected revenue drops:
      </p>
      <div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;padding:12px 16px;border-radius:8px;font-family:monospace;margin-bottom:12px;">
        DSCR = Annual EBITDA / Total Annual Principal &amp; Interest Payments
      </div>

      <h3>2. Maximum Borrowing Capacity Rule of Thumb</h3>
      <p>
        Most commercial institutions limit total term loan exposure to <strong>10% to 25% of annual gross sales</strong> or <strong>2.0x to 3.0x annual EBITDA</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5: Decision Framework -->
    <section class="content-section" id="cash-flow-strategy">
      <h2>Cash Flow vs. Total Interest: The Strategic Decision Framework</h2>
      <p>
        When deciding between loan offers, structure your financing around your business's return on invested capital (ROIC):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Choose the Longer Term (e.g. 10 Years) if:</strong> You are expanding into new markets, hiring key staff, or investing in inventory where maintaining liquidity and a lower monthly payment buffer is essential to survive ramp-up phases.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose the Shorter Term (e.g. 3–5 Years) if:</strong> You are purchasing high-margin replacement equipment with predictable cash flow and want to eliminate commercial debt as quickly as possible.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: FAQ Accordion -->
